1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:03,220
Look up on a clear night
and the stars seem close,

2
00:00:03,240 --> 00:00:05,340
scattered just above the rooftops.

3
00:00:05,620 --> 00:00:07,840
So how far away is the nearest one?

4
00:00:08,540 --> 00:00:13,580
Light, the fastest thing there is, takes
more than four years to cross the gap.

5
00:00:13,900 --> 00:00:15,660
And here is the strange part.

6
00:00:15,880 --> 00:00:17,900
You cannot see that star at all.

7
00:00:18,160 --> 00:00:19,740
Not without a telescope.

8
00:00:20,880 --> 00:00:23,880
In this film I will show
you which star it is,

9
00:00:23,880 --> 00:00:26,640
how anyone measured a distance that large,

10
00:00:26,740 --> 00:00:30,980
what that gap looks like if you shrink
the whole thing down to fit on Earth,

11
00:00:30,980 --> 00:00:33,100
and how long it would take to get there.

12
00:00:33,540 --> 00:00:37,800
The nearest star to the Sun
is called Proxima Centauri.

13
00:00:38,160 --> 00:00:44,280
It is a red dwarf, a small, cool star,
far smaller and dimmer than ours.

14
00:00:44,580 --> 00:00:49,640
That is why you will never spot it
by eye, even on the darkest night.

15
00:00:50,140 --> 00:00:53,480
It gives out less than one
percent of the Sun's light.

16
00:00:53,780 --> 00:00:56,680
But small stars burn their fuel slowly.

17
00:00:56,980 --> 00:01:00,760
Proxima will still be glowing
for trillions of years,

18
00:01:00,900 --> 00:01:03,600
long after our own Sun has gone.

19
00:01:04,300 --> 00:01:06,080
But it is not alone.

20
00:01:06,580 --> 00:01:13,460
Close beside it in the sky are two much
brighter stars, Alpha Centauri A and B,

21
00:01:13,680 --> 00:01:19,020
a pair a little further off at about
four point three seven light-years.

22
00:01:19,680 --> 00:01:22,240
From the southern hemisphere
they shine as one

23
00:01:22,240 --> 00:01:25,340
of the brightest points
in the whole night sky.

24
00:01:25,560 --> 00:01:28,200
From Britain, they never rise.

25
00:01:28,880 --> 00:01:30,940
The three are one family.

26
00:01:31,140 --> 00:01:35,240
Proxima loops around the bright
pair at a vast distance,

27
00:01:35,300 --> 00:01:39,120
and one orbit takes it
about half a million years.

28
00:01:39,780 --> 00:01:42,300
When anyone says "the nearest star",

29
00:01:42,400 --> 00:01:48,140
they mean the faint one: Proxima,
four point two four light-years away.

30
00:01:48,900 --> 00:01:52,280
A light-year is a distance, not a time.

31
00:01:52,500 --> 00:01:55,480
It is how far light travels in a year.

32
00:01:55,880 --> 00:01:57,940
So when you look towards Proxima,

33
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:01,720
you are seeing light that left
it more than four years ago.

34
00:02:02,140 --> 00:02:05,700
How do you measure the distance
to something you can never visit?

35
00:02:06,580 --> 00:02:07,980
Try this.

36
00:02:08,100 --> 00:02:10,740
Hold up your thumb at arm's length.

37
00:02:11,260 --> 00:02:13,800
Close one eye, then the other.

38
00:02:14,520 --> 00:02:17,160
Your thumb jumps against the background.

39
00:02:17,480 --> 00:02:20,600
The closer your thumb,
the bigger the jump.

40
00:02:21,400 --> 00:02:23,760
Your two eyes are a ruler.

41
00:02:24,360 --> 00:02:28,620
Astronomers use the same trick
with the whole of Earth's orbit.

42
00:02:29,100 --> 00:02:34,760
Photograph a star in January, and again
in July, from the other side of the Sun.

43
00:02:35,500 --> 00:02:42,080
A nearby star shifts a tiny amount against
the far more distant stars behind it.

44
00:02:42,700 --> 00:02:46,520
Measure the shift, and geometry
gives you the distance.

45
00:02:47,180 --> 00:02:49,980
The shift is unbelievably small.

46
00:02:50,300 --> 00:02:53,380
For centuries nobody could see it at all,

47
00:02:53,520 --> 00:02:58,060
and some astronomers took that as
proof that Earth must stand still.

48
00:02:58,880 --> 00:03:01,480
The real reason was the distance.

49
00:03:02,320 --> 00:03:10,120
In 1838 the astronomer Friedrich Bessel
measured it for a star called 61 Cygni.

50
00:03:10,680 --> 00:03:13,100
The angle he found was like the width of a

51
00:03:13,100 --> 00:03:16,740
coin seen from about
thirteen kilometres away.

52
00:03:17,440 --> 00:03:21,420
It was the first time anyone had
measured the distance to a star,

53
00:03:21,660 --> 00:03:24,860
and it showed how deep the sky really is.

54
00:03:25,780 --> 00:03:29,940
Today a European
spacecraft called Gaia has

55
00:03:29,940 --> 00:03:33,420
measured these shifts for
more than a billion stars.

56
00:03:34,100 --> 00:03:38,480
Proxima's is the largest of
all, because it is the nearest.

57
00:03:39,100 --> 00:03:44,240
Even so, it is like seeing a coin
from about five kilometres away.

58
00:03:44,940 --> 00:03:47,360
We make one of these every week.

59
00:03:47,980 --> 00:03:51,480
Subscribing is how the next one finds you.

60
00:03:52,960 --> 00:03:57,020
Numbers like four
light-years are hard to feel.

61
00:03:57,400 --> 00:03:59,460
So let's shrink everything down.

62
00:03:59,900 --> 00:04:03,480
Make the Sun the size of a
grapefruit, and put it on a table.

63
00:04:03,960 --> 00:04:09,960
At that scale, Earth is a pinhead about
fifteen metres away, across a big room.

64
00:04:10,480 --> 00:04:16,380
Everything you have ever known, every
person, every city, sits on that pin.

65
00:04:16,839 --> 00:04:19,480
The farthest any human has ever travelled,

66
00:04:19,480 --> 00:04:23,180
to the Moon, is only about
four centimetres from it.

67
00:04:23,860 --> 00:04:25,880
Now where is the nearest star?

68
00:04:26,380 --> 00:04:28,400
Proxima becomes a cherry.

69
00:04:28,560 --> 00:04:31,980
And that cherry is about four
thousand kilometres away.

70
00:04:32,560 --> 00:04:37,320
Put the grapefruit in London, and the
cherry is further away than Cairo.

71
00:04:37,800 --> 00:04:40,300
A little beyond it sit the bright pair,

72
00:04:40,340 --> 00:04:45,580
Alpha Centauri A and B: one about
the size of a big grapefruit,

73
00:04:45,580 --> 00:04:47,580
the other more like an orange.

74
00:04:47,880 --> 00:04:49,940
Between them, almost nothing.

75
00:04:50,200 --> 00:04:55,800
A few specks of dust, and an emptiness
so complete that it is hard to picture.

76
00:04:56,400 --> 00:04:58,460
And our farthest spacecraft?

77
00:04:58,900 --> 00:05:02,100
Voyager 1 launched in 1977.

78
00:05:02,360 --> 00:05:04,740
After nearly fifty years of flying,

79
00:05:04,740 --> 00:05:09,600
at this scale it has travelled about two
and a half kilometres from the grapefruit.

80
00:05:09,800 --> 00:05:11,760
It has barely left the town.

81
00:05:12,280 --> 00:05:14,800
So how long would the journey take?

82
00:05:15,260 --> 00:05:19,140
Walk at a steady pace, never
stopping, never sleeping,

83
00:05:19,260 --> 00:05:23,660
and you would reach Proxima in
about nine hundred million years.

84
00:05:24,020 --> 00:05:29,260
Take a car at motorway speed, and it
is about forty-six million years,

85
00:05:29,380 --> 00:05:32,020
far longer than humans have existed.

86
00:05:32,600 --> 00:05:36,560
A jet airliner cuts it to
about five million years.

87
00:05:36,980 --> 00:05:42,140
Voyager 1 is the fastest thing we have
ever sent out of the solar system,

88
00:05:42,280 --> 00:05:46,020
moving at about seventeen
kilometres every second.

89
00:05:46,160 --> 00:05:48,460
It is not heading towards Proxima.

90
00:05:48,480 --> 00:05:51,400
But at its speed, crossing
the same distance

91
00:05:51,400 --> 00:05:54,660
would take about
seventy-five thousand years.

92
00:05:55,220 --> 00:05:59,000
Even light, at three hundred
thousand kilometres a second,

93
00:05:59,020 --> 00:06:01,840
needs four years and three months.

94
00:06:02,160 --> 00:06:04,520
Put that beside the light you know.

95
00:06:04,924 --> 00:06:07,984
Moonlight reaches you
in just over a second.

96
00:06:08,184 --> 00:06:10,804
Sunlight takes about eight minutes.

97
00:06:11,044 --> 00:06:14,904
Light from the nearest star
takes more than four years,

98
00:06:14,904 --> 00:06:18,504
and that is the shortest
trip of its kind there is.

99
00:06:18,984 --> 00:06:21,604
So what is waiting at
the end of the journey?

100
00:06:21,904 --> 00:06:25,904
In 2016 astronomers found
a planet around Proxima.

101
00:06:26,384 --> 00:06:29,684
Proxima b is at least
about as massive as Earth,

102
00:06:29,824 --> 00:06:32,784
and it circles its star every eleven days,

103
00:06:32,844 --> 00:06:36,364
close enough to sit in the zone
where water could be liquid.

104
00:06:36,843 --> 00:06:39,244
But do not picture a second Earth yet.

105
00:06:39,404 --> 00:06:43,584
Nobody knows what its surface is
like, or whether it has air at all.

106
00:06:43,984 --> 00:06:46,044
Its star is a violent one.

107
00:06:46,084 --> 00:06:50,264
Proxima throws out flares,
sudden blasts of energy that,

108
00:06:50,264 --> 00:06:54,384
for a star its size, are far
more violent than our Sun's.

109
00:06:54,684 --> 00:06:57,464
They may have stripped
any atmosphere away.

110
00:06:58,124 --> 00:07:01,604
Whether Proxima b could ever
be friendly is still open.

111
00:07:01,904 --> 00:07:03,504
Could we ever go and look?

112
00:07:04,044 --> 00:07:06,984
One plan, called Breakthrough Starshot,

113
00:07:07,064 --> 00:07:10,463
imagines probes weighing
about as much as a paperclip,

114
00:07:10,484 --> 00:07:14,244
pushed by giant lasers to a
fifth of the speed of light,

115
00:07:14,304 --> 00:07:16,604
arriving in about twenty years.

116
00:07:16,904 --> 00:07:19,024
Nothing like it has been built yet.

117
00:07:19,124 --> 00:07:21,964
It is a plan, and a very hard one.

118
00:07:22,704 --> 00:07:25,164
Even a simple hello is slow.

119
00:07:25,504 --> 00:07:30,804
A radio message sent from Earth
tonight would arrive in early 2031.

120
00:07:31,284 --> 00:07:37,344
If someone there answered straight away,
the earliest reply would reach us in 2035.

121
00:07:37,864 --> 00:07:40,444
So this is the nearest star.

122
00:07:40,824 --> 00:07:43,424
A faint red sun you cannot see,

123
00:07:43,484 --> 00:07:48,384
more than four light-years away,
with a world we cannot yet know.

124
00:07:49,324 --> 00:07:51,344
What if the most important thing about the

125
00:07:51,344 --> 00:07:56,084
nearest star is not how
close it is, but how far?

126
00:07:56,744 --> 00:08:00,224
The bigger question sits
above you every clear night.

127
00:08:00,584 --> 00:08:04,464
Each of those points of light
is at least that far away,

128
00:08:04,664 --> 00:08:07,404
most of them far, far further.

129
00:08:07,804 --> 00:08:10,964
And we have only just
begun to measure the gap.

